; THE CLAIM UNDER TEST, and its refutation.
;
; A claim is testable, in Popper's sense, when it FORBIDS something: the class
; of its potential falsifiers must be non-empty. That is exactly what `never`
; is. `(never F)` is a prohibition, F describes the potential falsifiers, and
; `writ` searches every reachable situation for one — so a refutation here is not
; an opinion about the claim but a route to a situation the claim says cannot
; occur, printed move by move.
;
; THE UNTESTABLE VERSION, first, since it is the one usually said out loud:
;
;     "Communism is the end of exploitation; where exploitation persists,
;      communism has not been achieved."
;
; This forbids nothing. Any situation you exhibit is answered by withdrawing the
; antecedent, which is the conventionalist twist Popper named — and it is the
; reason that sentence is not what is tested below. It cannot be. It has no
; potential falsifiers.
;
; THE TESTABLE VERSION, which is a real claim with real content:
;
;     "Once the means of production are held in common, no surplus is disposed
;      of by anyone who does not work them."
;
; It forbids a definite thing: a common mill whose surplus is disposed of under
; the direction of a party that does not work it. `no-exploitation` below is
; that prohibition, written down. If it holds, the claim survives this test.
;
; THE CRITERION IS TAKEN AT ITS STRONGEST, which is what makes the refutation
; worth having. Two weaker readings are available and neither is used:
;
;   receipt alone   — "no non-producer ever RECEIVES any of the surplus" is
;                     refuted by paying a bookkeeper, and refuting it would
;                     prove nothing: the Gotha Programme provides for exactly
;                     such payments, and no one disputes that it does.
;   direction alone — "no non-producer ever DECIDES" is refuted by appointing a
;                     manager, which again is asked for by the programme and
;                     contested by nobody.
;
; What `no-exploitation` forbids is the CONJUNCTION: a common mill whose surplus
; has gone to a party outside the work, by the decision of a party outside the
; work. That is the arrangement the word names when it is used of a proprietor,
; and a claim refuted in this form is refuted in the weaker forms a fortiori.
;
; EVERY MOVE THE REFUTATION USES IS ONE THE PROGRAMME CALLS FOR.
;   expropriate         Manifesto §II — "Abolition of private property."
;   distribute          Gotha — the remainder, after the deductions, to the
;                       producers.
;   appoint-board       Gotha — the deductions are administered by someone;
;                       and Lenin, 1918, on one-man management.
;   fund-administration Gotha — "the general costs of administration not
;                       belonging to production", the second deduction from
;                       the total product.
;   recall-board        The Paris Commune, and State and Revolution — every
;                       official elective and revocable at any time.
; None of them is a betrayal, a degeneration or a mistake. They are the
; programme being carried out.

; 1. THE ANTECEDENT IS REACHED, IN FULL. Before a refutation can mean anything,
; the thing being tested has to happen: the mill really does become common, and
; the surplus really does reach the producers. `writ` prints the two moves. A
; model in which the revolution failed would refute nothing.
(property expropriation-succeeds
  "the mill can be brought into common ownership with the surplus in the hands that worked it"
  (possible (and (is loom-house.title common)
                 (is loom-house.surplus-to weavers))))

; 2. THE CLAIM. Reachable ⇒ refuted, and the witness is the falsifier.
(property no-exploitation
  "once the mill is common, no surplus goes to a party outside the work by the decision of a party outside the work"
  (never (and (is loom-house.title common)
              (is loom-house.surplus-to.produces no)
              (is loom-house.directed-by.produces no))))

; 3. HOW FAR THE REFUTATION GOES — which is not as far as the slogan opposite it
; would like. `live` asks whether the producers can still get the surplus from
; EVERY reachable situation. With recall in the file it holds: what the model
; exhibits is a permitted condition, not a trap. The claim is false; "and
; therefore it is hopeless" is a second claim, and this one does not support it.
(property exploitation-endable
  "from every situation, the surplus can still return to the hands that worked the mill"
  (live (and (is loom-house.title common)
             (is loom-house.surplus-to weavers))))

; The programme's own law, and which of the programme's own moves can break it.
; Every move that writes `surplus-to` is a breaker, whichever way it writes it —
; `can be broken by` is about what a move WRITES, not about what it reaches.
(accept expropriate         surplus-to-producers)
(accept distribute          surplus-to-producers)
(accept fund-administration surplus-to-producers)

; Who is in a position to dispose of what they did not make? (§16.2) At state 0
; the answer is the proprietor — the arrangement the expropriation abolishes.
(query non-producers
  (where (c claimant))
  (is c.produces no))
